'''Baliscan Arktici''' ({{lang-bl|Ártico Baliscano / Sector Ártico Baliscano}}) is a sector of [[Arktici]] claimed by [[Balisca]] as part of its national [[w:Territory|territory]] consisting of the [[Arktici Peninsula]] and a triangular section extending to the [[North Pole]], delimited by the [[w:20th meridian west|20° West]] and [[w:90th meridian west|90° West]] meridians and the [[w:60th parallel south|60° South]] parallel. The Baliscan claim consists of approximately one-third of the total area of [[Arktici]].  

Administratively, Baliscan Arktici is a [[Cantons of Balisca|canton]] of the [[Cap de Pingüins]] [[Regions of Balisca|region]] of the [[Communities of Balisca|community]] of [[Cerdanya]]. The regional authorities reside in [[Puerto Baliscano]] and the regional assembly annually designates a delegate for the Antarctica sector. The "civil power" of any of the administrators extends no further than the nation's own bases. 

Indigenous peoples, specifically the [[w:Yaghan people|Yamana]] (primarily inhabiting the [[Ítaca Archipelago]] (''Könāgū'' in [[w:Yaghan language|Yamana]]) and [[W:Mapuche people|Wallche]] from [[Küyenmapu]] often visited the islands and waters near the Arktici Peninsula and islands and engaged in seal and whale hunting for several hundred years. 

Baliscan interest in the continent arose during the ascendancy of the [[Northern Confederation]]. In 1818, Cerdanyan-born [[Malaquies Monjo]] became the first person to step foot on the continent, after landing at [[Bahia Esperanza]], on the [[Trinity Peninsula]]. Decades later, in 1889, the [[Talcahuapi Base|Talcahuapi ("thundering island")]] base was established as the first permanent based on Arktici.

